---
// How this site counts visits: the public account of what the Worker records
// (docs/database.md, docs/adr/0001-first-party-visitor-cookie.md). Keep it in
// step with what the Worker actually does.
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { site } from "../lib/site";
---

<Page
  title={`How this site counts visits | ${site.name}`}
  description="What this site records when you read or play something, and what it doesn't.">
  <article class="post privacy">
    <header class="post-head">
      <h1>How this site counts visits</h1>
    </header>

    <p>
      I like knowing whether anyone reads what I write or plays what I make. So the site keeps a small record of
      what happens on it. Here's all of it.
    </p>

    <h2>An anonymous id for your browser</h2>
    <p>
      The first time your browser does something here, the site gives it a cookie called <code>visitor</code>
      holding a random id, like <code>0b3c9d5e-1f2a-4b6c-8d7e-9f0a1b2c3d4e</code>. It's made up on the spot and
      says nothing about who you are. It lets me count how many different browsers visit in a day, a week or a
      month, and whether people come back.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>It lasts about 400 days and isn't renewed, so after that your browser gets a new one.</li>
      <li>It's only ever sent to this site. No other site, advertiser or tracker sees it.</li>
      <li>One person on a phone and a laptop counts as two browsers.</li>
      <li>Clearing your cookies for this site throws it away.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>No IP addresses</h2>
    <p>
      The site never stores your IP address, your location, your name or your email. Cloudflare, which hosts the
      site, handles every request the way any web host does, but nothing it sees is added to the record.
    </p>

    <h2>What gets recorded</h2>
    <p>Each thing below is one line in the record, with the page it happened on, the time, and the browser's id.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A View</strong>: opening any page of the site.</li>
      <li><strong>A Play</strong>: starting a run of a game.</li>
      <li><strong>A Finish</strong>: a run reaching its ending, and which ending.</li>
      <li><strong>A Like</strong>: pressing Like at the end of a post.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>An Interest</strong>: answering "I would" to whether you'd subscribe by email. No address is asked
        for.
      </li>
      <li><strong>A Click out</strong>: following a link from here to another site, such as GitHub.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each line also notes how you arrived: the <code>?ref=</code> tag on the link if it had one (like
      <code>?ref=bluesky</code>), else the name of the site that linked here, else "direct". Link previews, crawlers
      and other bots aren't counted.
    </p>

    <h2>How long it's kept</h2>
    <p>
      The counts are kept for good. The browser ids aren't: once a line is 13 months old, its id is wiped, so it
      can no longer be tied to anything else your browser did.
    </p>

    <h2>Other cookies</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>liked</code> and <code>interested</code> remember that you've already liked a post or answered, so you can't do it twice.</li>
      <li>
        My own browsers are recognised by their <code>visitor</code> id once I've signed in to my private page, so my
        visits don't count.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For a short while longer the pages also load Cloudflare Web Analytics, which counts visits without cookies.
      It's being removed now that the site counts for itself.
    </p>

    <h2>Who sees it</h2>
    <p>
      Only me, on a private page. The one number anyone else sees is how many times a post has been read, shown at
      its top. If you'd like to ask about any of this, <a href="/out/email/">email me</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Page>
